WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.853
Reliable object-level authorization is a
sequence of connected engineering

2
00:00:04.933 --> 00:00:05.592
decisions.

3
00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:09.959
Authenticate the requester, then construct
trusted subject, tenant, role,

4
00:00:10.039 --> 00:00:14.062
and permission context from
server-controlled authentication state.

5
00:00:14.142 --> 00:00:16.780
Validate identifiers and commands at
runtime.

6
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:21.609
Select candidate objects inside the
broadest scope the caller is permitted to reach.

7
00:00:21.689 --> 00:00:25.778
Apply an explicit policy when ownership,
membership, delegation, role,

8
00:00:25.858 --> 00:00:29.617
fields, relationships, or object state
require a richer decision.

9
00:00:29.697 --> 00:00:32.928
Preserve those constraints through the
read or mutation.

10
00:00:33.008 --> 00:00:36.174
Apply that sequence everywhere an object
can be reached:

11
00:00:36.254 --> 00:00:40.409
list and detail reads, updates, deletes,
nested resources, attachments,

12
00:00:40.489 --> 00:00:45.304
downloads, exports, alternate identifiers,
bulk operations, and workflow actions.

13
00:00:45.384 --> 00:00:47.428
Design route, service, repository,

14
00:00:47.508 --> 00:00:51.597
and policy interfaces so sensitive
operations cannot conveniently omit

15
00:00:51.677 --> 00:00:52.600
authorization.

16
00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.846
Test peer users, tenants, roles, nested
relationships,

17
00:00:55.926 --> 00:00:57.773
and every member of a collection.

18
00:00:57.853 --> 00:01:02.205
Review denied operations for both the
correct response and the absence of side

19
00:01:02.285 --> 00:01:02.813
effects.

20
00:01:02.893 --> 00:01:04.608
Keep four distinctions clear.

21
00:01:04.688 --> 00:01:09.238
Identifier unpredictability can reduce
discovery, but it is not authorization.

22
00:01:09.318 --> 00:01:11.693
A role check can permit an action
category,

23
00:01:11.773 --> 00:01:14.740
but it may not establish a relationship to
one object.

24
00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.580
TypeScript can make trusted context and
policy inputs consistent,

25
00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:20.902
but types do not enforce runtime access.

26
00:01:20.982 --> 00:01:23.290
Authentication can establish identity,

27
00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:27.129
but authenticated access is not
automatically authorized access.

28
00:01:27.209 --> 00:01:29.716
The practical standard is straightforward:

29
00:01:29.796 --> 00:01:34.148
every operation that uses a
client-influenced object reference must obtain a

30
00:01:34.228 --> 00:01:38.317
server-side decision for the authenticated
subject, requested action,

31
00:01:38.397 --> 00:01:39.848
and actual target object.

32
00:01:39.928 --> 00:01:44.083
When that rule is built into the data path
and reinforced by negative tests,

33
00:01:44.163 --> 00:01:48.648
missing authorization becomes visible in
design, difficult to express in code,

34
00:01:48.728 --> 00:01:51.300
and less likely to return during
maintenance.
